> After reading this, I took a look at xz-5.6.1.tar.bz2 and extracted the=20
> payload manually myself. The `sed \=E2=80=9Dr\n\=E2=80=9D $gl_am_configma=
ke` never=20
> worked for me, so instead I replaced that with cat and could still get=20
> the script extracted.
>=20
> However, the script I extracted has a diff: http://sprunge.us/okPUXN
>=20
> This seems to look for yet another test and if it exists extracts a=20
> shell script from yet another test - before even checking any of the=20
> abort conditions. I think the assumption "If you don't build an RPM /=20
> deb, you're fine" probably does not hold as a result.
>=20
> If I run those greps manually, I have no matches. So this could mean=20
> this is just future proofing for future tests to be checked in.=20
> However, I suspect that this is because I extracted the script without=20
> executing configure, so I'm guessing there is a transformation missing=20
> that would transform these greps to something else that would then=20
> match.
>=20
> Has anyone else looked into this in more detail? My impression is that=20
> everybody went by the initial analysis, assumed they are safe and=20
> didn't do any further reversing.
>=20
> Also I've looked at the .o that gets linked in. It's 88 KB in size and=20
> uses misleading symbols: They are symbols that actually exist in=20
> liblzma, but prefixed with .L, meaning they are local - and do=20
> something else entirely than the name implies. I did some static=20
> analysis, but then hit an indirect branch where I don't know where it=20
> goes. In any case, 88 KB is a lot for just a backdoor in SSH, so I'm=20
> wondering if it does more.
>=20
> I think there really needs to be more reverse engineering. Is there any=20
> such effort? I think it would make sense to join forces and start a=20
> group.=

